  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    The chamber beneath Midway Shelf had been chosen by the Cell, which told Niaecum~ everything she needed to know before anyone spoke. The fire-core brazier at room's center burned at forge-grade—extravagant, a ninety-day crystal wasted on a quarterly certification that might last two hours. Host's privilege, belonging to whoever controlled the warren below, which the Cell hadn't for two cycles. No one mentioned this. Sanctcateia~ had positioned herself at the brazier's near side, the orange glow catching the oil-sheen on her cracked amulet—the fracture running from the owl sigil to the fire-core inlay, visible only when the light pulsed at the right angle. She'd oiled it that morning. Niaecum~ could smell the resin from across the table: cedar-smoke and rendered fat layered over the chemical bite of fire-core condensate. The scent of a guild that had lost its warren and kept waxing its credentials.
    
    Niaecum~ had arrived second—deliberate, not early enough to seem eager, not late enough to insult—and taken the position midway between the brazier and the tunnel mouth, the acoustically superior space where voices from the warm side carried and died. Three exits. Nine bodies, not including the orca.
    
    The Gore Emissary filled the chamber's western alcove like a tongue fills a mouth. The Wake-Singer delegate had entered through the flooded access channel—the only way an orca body could navigate Nightshelf architecture—and now rested in the shallow pool carved for the purpose, water lapping stone, her bulk making the vaulted space feel like a burrow. Her headdress of carved bone caught the brazier's light. She had not spoken during the opening formalities, and her silence had more weight than anything the Cell's trade-speaker recited from the certified manifests.
    
    The manifests were fiction. Six hundred forty-two fire-core units, the trade-speaker read, down ninety-one from previous cycle due to operational challenges in the deep-forge sector. *Operational challenges* meaning the seal the Midnight Claws had poured over the lower access when the orca raiding party breached it—a seal no parliament body had authorized, applied with morite and crushed quartz by operatives who'd arrived before anyone could ask questions. The ninety-one missing units could have heated sixty Nightshelf dwellings through a Long Dark. Nobody asked where they'd gone.
    
    Sanctcateia~ confirmed the figures with a flip of her seal. Niaecum~ confirmed them on behalf of ☽'ofund' with a fractured seal of her own, because the guild's authentication had been under Shadow Parliament review for three cycles and every document they stamped was technically contraband. Two broken authorities certifying numbers compiled by a third party that wasn't present, for a warren controlled by a fourth party that nobody named.
    
    Then The Gore Emissary spoke. Her voice was a subsonic thing Niaecum~ felt in her sternum before her ears processed it: "The pod notes the eastern vents remain unmonitored."
    
    She let the stone's weight settle. Unmonitored vents meant uncounted heat-bleed, meant someone could be siphoning fire-core energy without registering it, meant the figures the Cell had just confirmed were built on a foundation nobody was watching. Accusation or offer. The grammar allowed both. The Gore Emissary let it sit with a patience that had nothing to do with diplomacy, and even Sanctcateia~ kept glancing at her, though she tried not to.
    
    &ast;
    
    After the session, in a side corridor too narrow for orca passage—a consideration that was not accidental—Sanctcateia~ found her.
    
    "Your guild's charter review," the Cell negotiator said. "Three cycles."
    
    "We're patient."
    
    "You're stalling. The Parliament won't restore authentication while your routes are formally unregistered. Every shipment you move is contraband by their codes, and eventually someone will enforce that."
    
    "Eventually," Niaecum~ agreed, and let the word settle.
    
    Sanctcateia~ leaned against the tunnel wall, the warm side where fire-core veins pulsed faintly behind the ice. "We could submit joint endorsement. Cell charter standing is still recognized—our qualification is unresolved, not revoked. Your routes gain authentication. Our claims gain geographic specificity."
    
    There it was. The Cell wanted ☽'ofund's tunnel maps. Not the trade routes—those were known, compromised, monitored by Midnight Claw checkpoints. The old routes. The ones ☽'ofund' runners had mapped during the Faction Wars, passages so deep and narrow that even the guild-master who'd carved *do not open* into the stone hadn't known all of them. Routes that bypassed every checkpoint between Junction Twelve and Ravium's eastern thermal vents.
    
    "Joint endorsement," Niaecum~ said. She studied Sanctcateia~. The amulet hung on its cord, that fresh-oiled crack turning it from insignia to confession. The chain showed years of wear beneath the morning's polish. Cell hardliners polished obsessively; pragmatists let the chains go dull. Sanctcateia~ was performing conviction.
    
    "What did you find in Ravium's deep-forge?"
    
    The pause lasted three heartbeats.
    
    "The eastern thermal yield requires coordinated extraction. A relic-class artifact surfaced during routine work. Deep-ice displacement from the lower veins. The Cell has initiated authentication protocols."
    
    "That's extraction labor," Niaecum~ said. "You need bodies in the deep-forge. You need Guild diggers to pull a relic out of corrupted ice because Cell operatives won't go back down there."
    
    Sanctcateia~'s flipper touched the cracked amulet once, briefly, the way a penguin touches a scar to confirm the wound is old. Then she said: "The orca." And stopped. She glanced toward the main chamber where The Gore Emissary had been and was no longer. "She wasn't listening to the figures. She was listening to the stone. The acoustics in Twelve carry—you know this. From where she sat, she could hear resonance patterns from every branching tunnel for sixty meters. My people have been tracking Wake-Singer movements below the fourth depth for nine days. They're not raiding. They're mapping."
    
    The temperature of the corridor seemed to change, though the fire-core veins kept their steady pulse.
    
    "The terms we could have accepted yesterday," Niaecum~ said.
    
    She left through the junction fork and did not look back.
    
    &ast;
    
    The flooded access channel smelled of brine and something older—rendered fat, whale-oil, the deep-pressure residue that clung to orca skin. Niaecum~ stood at the channel's stone lip, water lapping her feet, and waited. She did not wait long.
    
    The Gore Emissary surfaced without sound. For something that massive, the silence was obscene. Her headdress of ancestor-bone caught the faint bioluminescence of the channel walls. Niaecum~ kept to the pool's narrow end, where the ceiling dropped low enough that the orca could not follow without beaching herself.
    
    "You smell like their oil," The Gore Emissary said. The hunt-metaphors were absent. Between the two of them, in a tunnel no penguin had chosen as a meeting place, the courtesy-architecture fell away.
    
    "I've been in their corridor. And now in yours."
    
    "The small guild. The one that has nothing."
    
    "The one with tunnel-knowledge," Niaecum~ corrected, and watched the orca's breathing change. Barely. A fraction of rhythm.
    
    "The Cell says your chanters are mapping below the fourth depth. That you aren't here to negotiate. That your presence at this table is cover for something beneath it." She offered it the way one offers a trade-good for inspection.
    
    The Gore Emissary was silent for a long time. Water lapped at her scarred flank. Somewhere below them, deep enough that the sound arrived as vibration rather than noise, something shifted in Ravium's corrupted quarter.
    
    "Your Cell friends told you this so you would come to me. So I would deny it or confirm it. Either answer tells them which routes I know." A pause. "What does it tell *you*?"
    
    "It tells me whoever controls Ravium's eastern vents next season controls who freezes and who doesn't. The Cell can't retake it without routes they don't have. You can't hold the deep-forge without tunnel infrastructure your kind can't build. The Claws hold it now, and they're not at this table, which means they've already decided negotiation is finished." She breathed. The air tasted of sulfur and brine. "☽'ofund' Guild has no fleet, no fighters, no charter authentication. We have passage-knowledge through stone that no one else has walked. I am here to establish that we exist."
    
    "Exist."
    
    "That when whatever happens next reshapes Ravium, there is a name on the record. At the table."
    
    "You want to trade maps for a name."
    
    "I want to trade maps for the acknowledgment that my guild carried those maps when everyone else ran."
    
    Something moved behind the orca's dark eye—not sympathy, nothing so warm, but recognition. One diminished thing seeing another and measuring the distance between.
    
    "The deep stirs," The Gore Emissary said. "We feel it in the pressure-shifts. The corruption-scar at Ravium's center—your kind seal it and bore through it and pretend the deep is a resource. It is not a resource. Something sleeps in Ravium's roots. The Cell is pulling at its teeth."
    
    Niaecum~ felt the cold then—not the tunnel's ambient chill but the specific, pointed cold of understanding.
    
    "Show me a route," The Gore Emissary said. "One. Prove it leads where you claim."
    
    Niaecum~ reached into her satchel and produced a bone-marked tablet, ☽'ofund' cartography so fine the tunnel junctions were carved at the width of a feather. She set it on the pool's edge. The orca's eye followed it.
    
    She left it there. She did not take it back. She knew what the Shadow Parliament would call this, what the Cell's hardliners would carve into stone beside *VAULIUS KEPT THE FIRE HONEST*. She knew that once shared, a route could not be unshared—that The Gore Emissary's chanters would sing those passages into Wake-Singer memory, and no Guild vote or parliamentary review could extract the knowledge.
    
    She walked to the narrow end of the pool where the ceiling kept her safe and waited until The Gore Emissary's escort surfaced—two larger orcas who had held in the lower approaches during the session—and then she left Junction Twelve through routes that weren't on any tablet she'd ever give away.
    
    &ast;
    
    Three days later, a Cell courier carrying joint-endorsement terms for the Shadow Parliament was found in the Midway Shelf corridor with the message tube opened and resealed. The tube contained the original documents, unsigned, and a single bone chip etched with frequencies only orcas used—a hunt-song fragment that, when analyzed by a Cell acoustician working through the night, resolved into tunnel coordinates the Cell had never mapped.
    
    Coordinates Niaecum~ had provided.
    
    Sanctcateia~ sent a runner to Junction Twelve with a revised offer: eighty units, passage rights, joint stewardship of the eastern thermal vents. In exchange, exclusive Guild labor commitment to the deep-forge extraction. No independent alliance clauses. No third-party signatories. No Wake-Singers at the table.
    
    The runner found the junction empty. The brazier had been drawn down to embers, and the ice walls had stopped weeping.
    
    Sanctcateia~ sent a second runner. Then a third. The third did not return. On the fourth morning, a ☽'ofund' supply courier was found frozen in the Midway Shelf corridor, his manifest scattered, his fire-core allocation confiscated. No formal denial-of-trade registered with the Shadow Parliament. On the fifth, the Cell sealed the sub-fourth corridors with fire-core morite and crushed quartz. Official dispatches cited operational security.
    
    On the sixth day, the eastern thermal vents went dark.
    
    Niaecum~ stood in the dim of Nightfall Shelf and listened to the silence where the pipes had hummed. The aurora-crystal sconces—Guild-supplied, three cycles overdue for recharge—pulsed in the uneven rhythm that had become Nightshelf standard since the surface stopped exporting. She reached for the fire-core brazier at her station. It was cold.
    
    Below Junction Twelve, at ☽'ofund' Guild's formal trade-stall, the fractured seal remained pressed into the cold stone beside the door. Beneath it, someone had carved a single word in old-script, the kind that meant a name had been registered and could not be unstruck:
    
    *Niaecum~*
    
    The ice in Ravium's corrupted quarter began to sing at frequencies that made the bioluminescent pools go dark. In the flooded channels, Wake-Singer chanters were learning the geometry of penguin architecture, singing tunnel junctions into memory that would outlast every seal, every charter, every parliament that had ever claimed authority over stone. And in the deep-forge, where scorch-marks layered in three distinct patterns and the dark ice in the eastern corner showed things no one wanted to see, something that was not peace and not yet war shifted against its roots, and the careful arguments about operational challenges and quarterly certification and joint endorsement died in the mouths of those preparing to speak them.
    
    There was only what came next.
  CANONICAL_CHRONICLE
  annotation "note_1771585486627_0", anchor: "They're not raiding. They're mapping.", text: "Standard orca assault doctrine — shore-breakers driving access channels, herders pushing prey toward killing zones, finishers waiting below escape routes, the whole thing coordinated by hunt-song — depends entirely on knowing the target's architecture. The Gore Emissary sat in a penguin-built chamber reading sixty meters of branching tunnel through acoustic resonance. Her chanters below the fourth depth were singing that geometry into permanent Wake-Singer memory. The raid does not begin when they breach the walls. It began when someone carved that pool and invited them inside. Sanctcateia~ understood this. She told Niaecum~ anyway, because she needed ☽'ofund' more than she needed the information to stay secret. That calculation is how wars start.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771585486627_1", anchor: "☽'ofund' supply courier was found frozen in the Midway Shelf corridor", text: "Under the Flipper Accord, courier inviolability is absolute — the penalty is death by exposure. The seventeen skeletons at Midway Shelf exist to make this point, and every courier who walks past them knows it. A supply courier found frozen in that same corridor, manifest scattered, fire-core allocation confiscated, no denial-of-trade filed — this happened within sight of the bones. The earlier Cell courier's message tube opened and resealed is the same violation performed with more subtlety. Two couriers in one week, and no enforcement. The Accord's inviolability clause survived the Faction Wars. It did not survive this.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771585486627_2", anchor: "down ninety-one from previous cycle due to operational challenges", text: "Ninety-one. Specific enough to sound honest. But the eastern thermal vents' extraction capacity, per the deep-forge surveys still frozen in administrative ice, runs in multiples of thirteen. Ninety-one is seven thirteens. Someone reported exactly one full extraction cycle's yield as missing and dressed it in the appearance of granularity. I recognize the technique. I have used it on requisition forms when my assistants still existed to requisition things for.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771585486627_3", anchor: "Two broken authorities certifying numbers compiled by a third party", text: "The most concise description of late Clever Ice Age institutional governance I have read in forty-three annotation sessions. I may carve it into my desk.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771585486627_4", anchor: "Cell hardliners polished obsessively; pragmatists let the chains go dull", text: "Correct. The ice-memory from recovered Cell meeting caverns preserves distinct wear-pattern signatures on chain hardware — oxidation layering that varies with maintenance frequency. That this chronicler reads the distinction from a single observation suggests either deep familiarity with Cell internal culture or access to sources I would like to see cited.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771585486627_5", anchor: "She left it there. She did not take it back.", text: "Once Wake-Singer chanters sing those passages into memory, no seal, charter, or parliamentary vote can extract the knowledge. She traded permanent strategic intelligence for the right to have her name carved beside a door. I have spent a career watching institutions make this bargain — trading survival-critical assets for the acknowledgment that they existed. It is always the ones with nothing left. They are not always wrong.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771585486627_6", anchor: "Something sleeps in Ravium's roots. The Cell is pulling at its teeth.", text: "I have spent longer than anyone should cataloguing impressions frozen in ice older than penguin civilization. Attentions. Orientations. Patient presences that predate anything we have language for. I used to maintain scholarly distance about this. The Gore Emissary's metaphor — teeth, roots, sleeping — maps onto the deep-strata impressions with a specificity that unsettles me not because she is wrong, but because she arrived at the same reading I did through pressure-communion rather than ice-memory analysis. Two methods. Same conclusion. The Cell's 'relic-class artifact' may not be something they are excavating. It may be something they are waking up.", type: :tangent, display: :full
